= Ironic process theory — Simple =

'''Ironic process theory''' tries to explain why mental control can sometimes produce the opposite of what a person wants.

The theory says two processes can work together.

One process tries to find a different thought or mental state.

Another process keeps checking whether the unwanted thought is coming back.

That checking can keep the unwanted idea mentally active.

If a person is under heavy mental load, the effortful control process may weaken while the monitoring process keeps working.

This can make the unwanted thought easier to notice.

The theory is influential, but it does not mean every attempt at [[Thought suppression/Simple|thought suppression]] backfires.

